# SFT execution status v0.122: AWS native attempt, localized entry bug

2026-09-27 (Dhaka). **Gate 5 is still incomplete.** One generated-only native
attempt ran on a dedicated AWS CPU VM. The four-case tiny import/package chain
passed; full resource-probe acceptance failed. No real checkpoint/dataset was
acquired, no GPU was used, and no training or inference occurred.

The successor launcher now avoids an eager ML import before its resource
checks/probes. Its **42 local tests pass** (37 supervisor/source-copy tests,
2 fresh-entry regressions, 3 bootstrap regressions), with no skips. Ruff passes.
This corrected v3 launcher has **not run on Linux**. No retry of the started
native attempt was made, and no successful native acceptance is claimed.

## What actually ran

The user authorized using AWS credits and refreshed the default CLI login.
Only dedicated `t3.large` CPU instances were used in `us-east-1`: 2 vCPU, 8 GiB,
20-GiB encrypted gp3 root volume, standard CPU credits, no IAM instance profile,
IMDSv2 required, terminate-on-shutdown, delete-on-termination disk. AWS Pricing
API returned USD 0.0832 per Linux instance-hour; that is a rate, not a final bill.

Two bootstrap-only instances stalled before SSH/workload startup. The early
`systemd-run` call was the likely boot dependency problem; `--no-block` alone
did not resolve it. Both were terminated. The final v0.3 bootstrap has no early
boot command: it arms a boot-relative one-hour timer during cloud-init's final
stage, with a 15-second command timeout. That VM finished cloud-init and its
timer was directly observed `active/waiting` before staging and dispatch.
Final-stage boot succeeded; an exact trace of the earlier deadlocks was not
captured, so the report does not claim more precise proof of their mechanism.

Public SSH timed out despite the narrow operator-IP rule and an allowing route/
ACL configuration. A temporary AWS Instance Connect endpoint provided an
authenticated HTTPS tunnel. Its SG permitted SSH only to the new VM's SG.
The SSH ED25519 key was pinned from authenticated EC2 console output. No broad
public SSH rule, S3 permission or existing-instance change was used.

The VM used Canonical Ubuntu 24.04, systemd 255, signed-apt Python 3.12.3, and
34 exact hash/size-pinned CPU wheels (248,715,637 downloaded software bytes).
Versions included torch 2.14.0+cpu, transformers 5.15.1, safetensors 0.8.0,
tokenizers 0.22.2 and numpy 2.5.3. This is **not** the old OCI Python 3.12.14
image identity; the runtime difference is recorded, not silently equated.

## Source ownership correction before the native attempt

The immutable staged source is root-owned, while the unchanged import kernel's
`ContentIO.read_control` requires source-control files to share the worker UID.
This conflict was identified before native dispatch. v2 therefore makes exact,
hash-checked worker-owned copies of the source closure under its private
64-MiB `/work` tmpfs, then launches the fixed four-test child interpreter.
Original source files are neither chowned nor modified; importer checks stay
unchanged. The copy is exclusive, flat-path-allowlisted, bounded to 2 MiB, and
read back for hash verification. The source-copy/supervisor tests passed 37/37.

The first source bundle also independently ran the four actual integration
tests from extracted files, without reading the repository's original modules.
An initial local audit harness misinterpreted relative dir-fd opens against the
old cwd; switching the harness cwd to the extracted stage fixed that false
rejection. No application code or test assertions were relaxed for it.

## Native outcome and correction to intermediate status

Attempt: `67f4ec061b9f4728a1cfb14c52a4f112`, v2 manifest
`2acf79b7bec358aa643e04df5cd200c1dc9cab976a0c781a7fd1d3876a9e3420`.
The retained failure record says `case_failed`, `completed_cases=3`,
`retry_allowed=false`. This is a controller observation, **not three fully
qualified containment probes**.

| Case | Observation | Accepted interpretation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Import/package chain | Controller accepted fixed four-test child success | Generated-only tiny chain passed |
| Timeout | systemd `timeout`, signal 15, empty cgroup | Deadline acted; descendant probe readiness not established |
| Memory | systemd `oom-kill`, signal 9, empty cgroup | RAM cap acted; intended allocation probe readiness not established |
| Disk | `oom-kill`, not expected ENOSPC success, empty cgroup | Failed; no disk-capacity acceptance |

All four cgroups were confirmed absent after controller cleanup. Worker stdout/
stderr remained null. Only fixed result fields and kernel memory counters were
retained. Disk OOM showed 260,764 KiB anonymous RSS and zero shared-memory RSS;
this was not evidence of filling the intended 64-MiB tmpfs.

The root cause in the entry path is concrete: importing the control module as
`lumi.posttraining_import_vm_rehearsal_v2` executes `lumi/__init__.py`, which
auto-registers the installed Transformers/PyTorch stack before `worker_check`.
Small probe limits can therefore fire during ML imports rather than during
their intended probes. The intermediate user update calling three cases passed
was corrected when this was discovered.

v3 loads its stdlib-only control file with `importlib.util` without importing
the `lumi` package. Only the fixed chain child imports ML, after boundary checks
and worker-owned staging. A fresh-interpreter regression explicitly rejects
any `lumi`, torch, transformers, numpy or safetensors import while loading the
control entry. The original model package and all import/kernel/package/SFT
runtime sources remain byte-for-byte unchanged. The memory/disk/time ceilings
are unchanged. This addresses the identified bug but still needs native proof.

## Cleanup and retained evidence

All three created instances are confirmed `terminated`, all three temporary
EBS volumes are absent, the Instance Connect endpoint is `delete-complete`,
and the two temporary security groups and AWS SSH-key registration are absent.
Ephemeral VM files are no longer recoverable; the source archives, exact
started/failure records and aggregate diagnostics are retained locally under
`artifacts/lumi-sft-aws-rehearsal-attempt-v0.1/` and the versioned source bundles.
The local SSH private key is mode 0600 under a mode-0700 git-ignored artifact
directory; its AWS key registration has been deleted. No secret was printed.

## Next step, without expanding the roadmap

Obtain approval for **one fresh generated-only AWS CPU native attempt with the
corrected v3 launcher**, preserving this failed attempt and forbidding an
automatic retry. Use the working final-stage bootstrap and HTTPS tunnel, the
same resource ceilings, one-hour expiry and immediate cleanup. The prepared
source bundle is `artifacts/lumi-sft-aws-rehearsal-source-v0.3/` (161,791 bytes).

After successful native acceptance, remaining Gate-5 work is the real-input/
full-size coordinator adapter and independently verified real checkpoint
import. Those are not authorized or demonstrated by this generated rehearsal.
Gate 6 still needs its training-specific pre-dispatch approval, GPU smoke/
save/resume acceptance and SFT run; later evaluation and serving gates remain.
Production eligibility remains false. Nothing was committed or pushed.

References for the setup behavior:
[systemd timer dependencies](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/systemd/systemd/v255/man/systemd.timer.xml),
